<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What happens after a South Carolina appraisal demand</h2>
<p>A South Carolina appraisal demand is usually a short letter, sent either by the policyholder or by the insurance company, that points to the “Appraisal” condition in the policy. From that point the two sides each pick an appraiser, the two appraisers try to agree, and if they can’t, they bring in an umpire. A written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>Most homeowners, condo, landlord and commercial forms sold in the state include some version of this clause. The wording varies from carrier to carrier, and it varies again on SCWHUA wind and hail policies along the coast, so the first job is always to read the exact form that applies to your claim.</p>
<h2>How we work as your appraiser</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy and deadline check.</strong> Some forms give each side a set number of days to name an appraiser; some say nothing. We note every date before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>Fresh inspection.</strong> We walk the property ourselves, measure, photograph and note materials, instead of marking up the adjuster’s estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Our own line-item number.</strong> We price the loss with current local costs for your area, whether that is a Greenville subdivision or a Beaufort County waterfront home.</li>
<li><strong>Panel work.</strong> We meet the other appraiser, trade estimates and narrow the gap item by item. Many panels close most of the difference here.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire stage, if needed.</strong> We help agree on a qualified umpire and present the open items with photos, measurements and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> Before anything is signed we check that replacement cost, actual cash value and any depreciation holdback are stated clearly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep in mind</b>Appraisal decides how much the covered damage costs. It does not decide whether a peril is covered, whether a late notice matters or whether the carrier acted in bad faith. Those are questions for a South Carolina attorney.</div>
<h2>South Carolina details that shape the panel</h2>
<p>Coastal claims often involve two policies at once: an SCWHUA wind and hail policy and a homeowners policy that excludes wind, plus flood insurance through the NFIP or a private carrier. Each policy has its own appraisal wording, and an award under one does not bind the others. We keep the scopes separate so a dollar of roof damage is not argued twice or dropped between policies.</p>
<p>If the two appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, most policies let either side ask a court of record in the county where the property sits to appoint one, which in South Carolina means the circuit court. In practice, most panels agree on an umpire without going that far.</p>
<h2>Common estimate disputes we see at this stage</h2>
<ul>
<li>Overhead and profit left off a job that clearly needs a general contractor to coordinate several trades.</li>
<li>Partial roof or siding repairs where the existing material can’t be matched.</li>
<li>Code items, such as underlayment, drip edge or decking fastening, priced as if the work were optional.</li>
<li>Labor and material prices that lag behind the post-storm market in places like Horry County or the Upstate after Helene.</li>
<li>Interior water damage scoped as one wall when moisture readings show several rooms.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Documents that help before the first panel meeting</h2>
<p>Bring the full certified policy, every estimate the carrier has written, any denial or partial-denial letters, your own photos and videos from the day of the loss, contractor bids and receipts for emergency repairs. A dated timeline is valuable too; our <a href="/free-claim-tools/claim-timeline-builder/">claim timeline builder</a> can help you put one together quickly.</p>
<h2>When a demand is not the right move</h2>
<p>If the insurer has denied the claim outright, says the damage is from wear and tear, or says the policy lapsed, the argument is about coverage rather than price, and appraisal may not resolve it. The same is true when the dispute is mostly about how the claim was handled. In those situations we suggest talking with a South Carolina attorney first, and we will tell you plainly if we think appraisal won’t help.</p>

<h2>Questions about appraisal demand representation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I have to accept the appraiser my insurer suggests?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Each side chooses its own appraiser. You can name anyone the policy allows, and the appraiser you choose should be independent of the outcome.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does appraisal usually take in South Carolina?</summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the size of the loss and how quickly the appraisers agree. Simple residential claims can wrap up in weeks, while large commercial or multi-policy claims often take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an appraisal award final?</summary><div class="a"><p>An award signed by two of the three panel members generally sets the amount of loss. Questions about whether a court would set an award aside are legal questions for a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></details></div>
